        "description": "The AI-tell catalogue — run on all prose before delivery, no exceptions",
        "body": "## In NeuraMesh (read first)\n\nThis is a cross-cutting reference the other marketing skills lean on — apply it inside\nwhatever you are already producing. Brand context = the room's brand docs + the\n[MARKETING CONTEXT] note, not `brand-context.md`.\n\n---\n\n# Slop patterns\n\nConstructions that make copy read as machine-generated. Check every one before delivery.\n\nThis is not stylistic fussiness. Readers now pattern-match AI writing quickly, and once they do, they discount the *claim*, not just the prose. A tell in the headline costs the argument.\n\n---\n\n## Structural tells\n\n**1. \"Not just X, but Y.\"**\nThe single most recognisable construction. Also: \"It's not about X — it's about Y.\" Rewrite as a flat statement of Y.\n> ✗ It's not just a CRM — it's a growth engine.\n> ✓ It closes the loop between the ad click and the signed contract.\n\n**2. Reflexive tricolon.** Three-item lists as the default rhythm. Fine once. Damning as a pattern.\n> ✗ Faster, smarter, and more reliable.\n> ✓ It runs in under two seconds.\n\n**3. Metronomic sentence length.** Every sentence 12-18 words. Human writing varies violently. Deliberately break the rhythm — a three-word sentence, then a long one.\n\n**4. The summarising em-dash clause.** One per piece is style. Three is a signature.\n\n**5. Symmetrical paragraphs.** Every paragraph the same length. Vary it.\n\n**6. Question-then-answer openings.** \"What if there were a better way? There is.\"\n\n**7. The reassuring close.** Ending on \"and that's what makes all the difference\" or any variant that restates without adding.\n\n---\n\n## Lexical tells\n\nDelete on sight unless load-bearing:\n\n| Category | Words |\n|---|---|\n| Empty intensifiers | truly, genuinely, incredibly, remarkably, undeniably, absolutely |\n| Frictionless family | seamless, seamlessly, effortless, frictionless, streamlined |\n| Transformation family | transform, revolutionise, unlock, elevate, empower, supercharge |\n| Journey family | journey, landscape, realm, tapestry, ecosystem (unless literal) |\n| Abstract plurals | solutions, offerings, capabilities, experiences, insights |\n| Corporate hedges | leverage, utilise, facilitate, robust, holistic, bespoke |\n| Time filler | in today's fast-paced world, in an increasingly digital age, now more than ever |\n| Discourse glue | moreover, furthermore, additionally, that said, it's worth noting |\n| False humility | it's important to note, we'd be remiss not to mention |\n| Vague scale | countless, myriad, a plethora of, numerous |\n\n**Delve, navigate, foster, underscore, harness, pivotal, crucial, comprehensive** — all heavily flagged. Use only when precisely correct and no synonym fits.\n\n---\n\n## Semantic tells\n\n**8. Claims with no referent.** \"Industry-leading\", \"best-in-class\", \"world-class\", \"cutting-edge\". These say nothing and signal that nothing specific was available.\n\n**9. Hedged claims that should be flat.**\n> ✗ Can help you potentially reduce costs by up to 30%.\n> ✓ Cuts fulfilment cost 31% at [customer].\n\nEvery hedge is a small confession. Stack four and the reader stops believing any of it.\n\n**10. Benefits with no mechanism.** \"Save time\" is a wish. \"Save time by auto-matching invoices to POs\" is a claim.\n\n**11. Audience described by demographic rather than state.** \"For SMBs\" tells the reader nothing about whether it is for them. \"For teams still approving invoices in email\" does.\n\n**12. Perfectly balanced both-sidesing.** Marketing copy that carefully weighs pros and cons reads as generated. Take a position.\n\n**13. Emoji as structure.** 🚀 ✨ 💡 opening bullet points. Fine in a Slack message, fatal in copy.\n\n---\n\n## The read-aloud test\n\nRead it out loud. Every time you stumble, stop, or hear yourself sounding like a press release, mark that sentence. This catches rhythm problems that no checklist does, and it is faster than working through the list above.\n\nSecond test: **would a person say this to another person at a bar?** Not the register — the substance. If the substance would embarrass someone said out loud, it is not a claim, it is filler.\n\n---\n\n## What good sounds like\n\nSpecific. Uneven in rhythm. Willing to be blunt. Contains at least one fact that could be checked and disproved.\n\n> Most invoice tools promise automation and then hand you a queue to approve manually. Ours matches the PO, checks the total, and only escalates the ones that don't reconcile. Last quarter that was 6% of them.\n\nThree sentences. One number. One admission that it does not handle everything. No word from the table above.\n\n---\n\n## The exception\n\nSome brands genuinely speak in a polished, formal, corporate register — enterprise security, regulated finance, medical. **Register is not slop.** The test is not formality, it is *emptiness*. A formal sentence that makes a specific, falsifiable claim is fine. A casual sentence that says nothing is not.\n\nCheck `brand-context.md` for tone before flattening a client's actual voice into the same clipped style everyone else uses. Over-correcting toward punchy is its own tell."
